import{_ as s,o as a,c as t,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BS0bAV7E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"OCIRE - OpenCubeOS Independent Recovery Environment","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"dev/ocire.md","filePath":"dev/ocire.md"}'),o={name:"dev/ocire.md"};function i(r,e,c,l,d,p){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n(`<h1 id="ocire-opencubeos-independent-recovery-environment" tabindex="-1">OCIRE - OpenCubeOS Independent Recovery Environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#ocire-opencubeos-independent-recovery-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OCIRE - OpenCubeOS Independent Recovery Environment&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Status: <strong>3.0.0-WP-02-ocire_recovery</strong> (this work package ships the recovery features: the boot supervisor with watchdog-based boot-failure detection, and the full recovery shell with syscheck / repair).</p><h2 id="what-ocire-is" tabindex="-1">What OCIRE is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-ocire-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What OCIRE is&quot;">​</a></h2><p>OCIRE is a second L0 kernel, fully independent of the Open Cube OS (OCOS) kernel. It lives in its own source tree (<code>ocire/</code>), shares no headers with OCOS, links with its own linker script (<code>ocire/linker_ocire.ld</code>) and produces its own boot image (<code>build/ocire/ocire.iso</code>). The two-kernel architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>L0 = provides services</strong> — OCOS stays the full-service kernel. OCIRE is an L0 <em>peer</em>: a recovery kernel that boots when OCOS cannot.</li><li><strong>Independent</strong> — OCIRE must be able to boot, run and power-cycle the machine without any OCOS code, data or partition being reachable or required.</li></ul><h2 id="what-wp-01-ships" tabindex="-1">What WP-01 ships <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-wp-01-ships" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What WP-01 ships&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>WP-01</th><th>WP-02 (this)</th><th>WP-03</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Boot + minimal shell</td><td><strong>done</strong></td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>help / shutdown / reboot</td><td><strong>done</strong></td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery features, FAT12, self-check, repair</td><td>—</td><td><strong>here</strong></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Boot-failure detection, 1-minute timeout</td><td>—</td><td><strong>here</strong></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>OCOS &lt;-&gt; OCIRE interface, process protection</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>here</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-wp-02-ships" tabindex="-1">What WP-02 ships <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-wp-02-ships" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What WP-02 ships&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>The boot supervisor</strong> (<code>ocire.mode=sup</code>, the dual-boot menu default): scans the disks, runs the mailbox state machine, arms the i6300esb watchdog for a 60 s boot window, stages and digest-verifies the ring-3 log agent, then kexecs the OCOS kernel with the mailbox / log / wdt facts on the command line (<code>oc.log= oc.logsecs= oc.mb= oc.wdt= oc.agent=1</code>).</li><li><strong>Boot-failure detection</strong>: a watchdog reset that lands the supervisor on a still-<code>WAIT_BOOT_SIGNAL</code> mailbox means OCOS never came up - the supervisor prints the crash notice, bumps <code>fail_count</code>, sets <code>ENTER_FULL</code> and reboots into the full recovery shell. Proven end to end in QEMU with the real i6300esb device (<code>-device i6300esb</code>): arm -&gt; kexec -&gt; 60 s expiry -&gt; reset -&gt; crash notice -&gt; recovery shell.</li><li><strong>The full recovery shell</strong> (menu entry 2, or <code>ocire.mode=full</code>): <code>logread</code> (dump the boot-log ring), <code>syscheck</code> (ten-point integrity self-check), <code>repair quick</code> (fix MBRs / mailbox / log header + restore any system file whose digest differs from the golden manifest), <code>repair full</code> (DESTRUCTIVE reformat of the system volume + full golden rewrite, typed-YES confirmation), <code>return</code> (normal-boot mailbox + reboot), plus the WP-01 <code>help/shutdown/reboot</code>.</li><li><strong>FAT12 read / FAT32 read+write</strong>: the streaming helpers (<code>ocire_fat_read_range</code>, <code>ocire_fat_hash_file</code>, <code>ocire_fat_copy_stream</code>) let the recovery shell hash and restore multi-megabyte files without buffering them; allocation carries a reservation marker so two consecutive allocations can never hand out the same cluster, and names that do not fit the 8.3 form are rejected rather than silently truncated.</li></ol><h3 id="honest-boundary-wp-03-scope-by-design" tabindex="-1">Honest boundary (WP-03 scope, by design) <a class="header-anchor" href="#honest-boundary-wp-03-scope-by-design" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Honest boundary (WP-03 scope, by design)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The OCOS side of the closed loop is <strong>not</strong> in this work package: OCOS does not yet parse the <code>oc.*</code> command-line facts, does not write the <code>SIGNAL_OK</code> mailbox state, does not disarm/ping the i6300esb, and does not spawn the injected agent (the <code>L1SVC_CAT_OCIRE</code> channel the agent calls does not exist yet). Consequences, measured and declared:</p><ul><li>On real hardware with an i6300esb present, a supervised boot that reaches OCOS still hard-resets 60 s after hand-over (nobody disarms the window) - entry 3 &quot;Open Cube OS (direct boot)&quot; is the interim path for normal use.</li><li>Without the watchdog (the default QEMU setup), the mailbox-only signalling means every second consecutive supervised boot degrades into the recovery shell (un-signalled = presumed crash). The recovery shell&#39;s <code>return</code> resets the mailbox to NONE.</li></ul><h2 id="source-layout-ocire" tabindex="-1">Source layout (ocire/) <a class="header-anchor" href="#source-layout-ocire" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Source layout (ocire/)&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>ocire/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_mb2_header.S    multiboot2 header (80x25 text mode request)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_boot.S          32-bit GRUB entry -&gt; GDT + 4 GiB identity map</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        -&gt; long mode -&gt; ocire_main; exception stubs</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_main.c          kernel entry: console, IDT, ACPI, shell</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_console.c/.h    dual console: COM1 115200 + VGA text 0xB8000</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_input.c/.h      serial + PS/2 polled input (shift/caps/backsp)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_idt.c/.h        minimal IDT: 32 exception vectors, faults</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        print a diagnostic instead of triple-faulting</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_acpi.c/.h       RSDP/XSDT/FADT (checksums verified), tiny \\_S5</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        package reader, PM1a S5 + RESET_REG + 8042</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_mb2.c/.h        multiboot2 info parsing (framebuffer/cmdline/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        modules - WP-02 group 1)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_font.c/.h       embedded 8x16 font for the LFB console</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_ata.c/.h        ATA PIO driver (IDENTIFY, LBA28 read/write)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_sha256.c/.h     SHA-256 (golden/agent/kernel digests)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_fat.c/.h        FAT12 read + FAT32 read/write (mount, lookup,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        streaming read/hash/copy, create/mkdir/format)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_wdt.c/.h        Intel 6300ESB watchdog (PCI 8086:25ab probe,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        arm/disarm/ping, 1 kHz two-stage window)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_bootsvc.c/.h    the boot supervisor (mailbox state machine,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        agent staging, kexec hand-over)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_kexec.c/.h      OCOS ELF staging + mb2 info rebuild + jump</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_kexec_jump.S    64-bit kexec trampoline</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_disk.c/.h       recovery-disk discovery, mailbox, golden image</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_recover.c/.h    the full recovery shell commands (logread/</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        syscheck/repair/return)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_shell.c/.h      the command loop (8 commands)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_util.c/.h       freestanding mem/string helpers</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_io.h            port I/O inline helpers</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire_types.h         base scalar types</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  agent/ocire_agent.c   the ring-3 log agent (OCOS L1 ABI, injected</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        at kexec; its L1SVC channel is WP-03)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  ocire.mk              build targets (included from the top Makefile)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  linker_ocire.ld       own link layout (1 MiB load address)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  grub-ocire.cfg        standalone ISO menu (single entry)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  grub-dual.cfg.in      dual-boot disk menu TEMPLATE (3 entries:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>                        supervisor default, full shell, direct OCOS)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Build targets: <code>make ocire-kernel</code>, <code>make ocire-iso</code>, <code>make ocire-disk</code>, <code>make ocire-test</code>, <code>make ocire-dual-test</code>, <code>make ocire-shell-test</code> (run targets <code>ocire-run-bios/uefi[-gui]</code> are also available). Compilation uses the same <code>-Wall -Wextra -Werror</code> zero-warning gate as the OCOS kernel; CI runs the OCIRE build on every push. The OCIRE object files carry their own <code>-include $(OCIRE_OBJ_C:.o=.d)</code> header-dependency tracking in ocire.mk (the top-level Makefile only includes the OCOS kernel&#39;s .d files - a header change in ocire/ used to silently link stale objects; found and fixed live by the WP-02 battery).</p><h2 id="how-the-two-oses-coexist-wp-01-layout" tabindex="-1">How the two OSes coexist (WP-01 layout) <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-two-oses-coexist-wp-01-layout" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the two OSes coexist (WP-01 layout)&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>hd0 (ocire-boot.img, built by tools/make_ocire_disk.sh)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  MBR + GRUB (BIOS: boot.img + core.img in the post-MBR gap;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>              UEFI: /EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI on the FAT32 partition)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  p1 FAT32: /boot/opencube.elf (OCOS kernel)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>            /boot/ocire.elf    (copy of the OCIRE kernel)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>            /boot/grub/grub.cfg (the dual menu)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -&gt; GRUB menu entry 1 boots OCOS (default)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -&gt; GRUB menu entry 2 boots OCIRE</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>hd1 (ocire-hd.img) - the OCIRE disk</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  MBR with ONE entry, type 0xEE (&quot;GPT protective shadow&quot;), no GPT</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  header behind it; the raw OCIRE kernel image is stored at the</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  partition start (LBA 2048).</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -&gt; the GRUB menu loads it with a whole-disk blocklist:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>     multiboot2 (hd1)2048+128</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -&gt; OCOS refuses the entire partition table (0xEE makes its parser</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>     switch to GPT mode, which fails without a GPT header), so the</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>     OCIRE partition has no device node and no filesystem path in</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>     OCOS - see &quot;Partition isolation&quot; below.</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Two lessons from bring-up, encoded here for the record:</p><ol><li>GRUB 2.12&#39;s msdos partmap <strong>skips</strong> 0xEE entries — a partition path like <code>(hd1,msdos1)</code> does not exist for GRUB even though the same entry is perfectly valid on disk. The menu therefore uses a whole-disk blocklist.</li><li>GRUB menus have no digit hotkeys; automation selects entries with the ANSI cursor-down sequence over the serial menu terminal.</li></ol><h2 id="partition-isolation-the-wp-01-acceptance-test" tabindex="-1">Partition isolation (the WP-01 acceptance test) <a class="header-anchor" href="#partition-isolation-the-wp-01-acceptance-test" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Partition isolation (the WP-01 acceptance test)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Measured in <code>evidence/3_0_0_wp01_ocire/isolation.log</code> from inside a running OCOS with both disks attached:</p><ul><li><code>partscan: 1 partition device(s) registered</code> — the OCIRE disk contributes <strong>zero</strong> child devices (only the dual-boot disk&#39;s p1 registers).</li><li><code>parted hda</code> (OCIRE disk) → <code>no partition table found</code>.</li><li><code>mkfs.fat32 hdap1 confirm</code> → <code>device not found</code> — there is no node to write through.</li><li><code>disk_rw_test hdap1</code> → <code>device lookup FAIL</code> — there is no node to read through.</li><li><code>df</code> — only OCOS volumes are mounted; nothing from the OCIRE disk.</li></ul><p>Honest boundary (also measured, <code>isolation_rawdecl.log</code>): whole-disk device nodes still exist — <code>disk_rw_test hda</code> and <code>mkfs.fat32 hda confirm</code> both work on a throwaway copy, exactly like Linux root with <code>/dev/sdb</code>. Blocking administrator-level whole-disk operations requires an OCOS block-layer policy — an OCOS kernel change this work package explicitly forbids — and belongs to WP-03 (process protection).</p><h2 id="tests" tabindex="-1">Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>tools/run_ocire_tests.py</code> (<code>make ocire-test</code>) — 14 cases, BIOS + UEFI: boot to the shell prompt, <code>help</code>, unknown-command hint, backspace editing, <code>shutdown</code> (ACPI S5; QEMU really exits) and <code>reboot</code> (8042/RESET_REG; with <code>-no-reboot</code> QEMU really exits).</li><li><code>tools/run_ocire_dual_tests.py</code> (<code>make ocire-dual-test</code>) — 11 cases: the four dual-boot combinations (BIOS/UEFI x menu default supervisor-&gt;kexec-&gt;OCOS / menu-pick OCIRE full shell) on throwaway disk copies (the supervisor is stateful - see the WP-03 boundary), the five isolation probes and the two whole-disk declaration probes.</li><li><code>tools/run_ocire_shell_tests.py</code> (<code>make ocire-shell-test</code>) — the WP-02 recovery battery, 4 cases on throwaway copies: <code>shell_healthy</code> (syscheck 10/10 + idempotent quick repair on a pristine disk), <code>shell_quick_repair</code> (kernel-ELF byte flipped -&gt; syscheck catches the digest mismatch -&gt; repair quick restores + verifies -&gt; healthy again), <code>shell_full_repair</code> (p1 BPB nuked -&gt; boot from the STANDALONE ISO, hd0&#39;s own GRUB cannot read its config any more -&gt; repair full rewrites the volume from the golden image -&gt; healthy) and <code>shell_wdt_timeout</code> (the real i6300esb attached: supervisor arms the 60 s window -&gt; kexec -&gt; OCOS boots but never signals -&gt; watchdog reset -&gt; crash notice -&gt; the full recovery shell).</li><li>Full OCOS regression matrix on a tree that contains OCIRE but zero OCOS edits: <code>evidence/3_0_0_wp01_ocire/regression/</code> (host tests, reproducible-check, wp12, wp10_tui, chinese, tuiqa x2, native x2, l1_per_app, wait4 fresh-boot battery 20/20, boot gates) — all green and identical to the 2.2.0 release baseline.</li></ul>`,25)])])}const m=s(o,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
